label obo:CL_0002511 \"CD11b-low, CD103-negative, langerin-negative lymph node dendritic cell\")\n19441:# Class: obo:CL_0002512 (CD11b-high, CD103-negative, langerin-negative lymph node dendritic cell)\n19443:AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"GOC:tfm\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"http://www.immgen.org/index_content.html\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_0002512 \"A langerin-negative lymph node dendritic cell that is CD103-negative and CD11b-high.\")\n19446:AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label obo:CL_0002512 \"CD11b-high, CD103-negative, langerin-negative lymph node dendritic cell\")\n19459:# Class: obo:CL_0002514 (Vgamma5-negative CD8-alpha alpha positive gamma-delta intraepithelial T cell)\n19465:AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label obo:CL_0002514 \"Vgamma5-negative CD8-alpha alpha positive gamma-delta intraepithelial T cell\")\n19574:# Class: obo:CL_0002526 (CD14-positive dermal dendritic cell)\n19576:AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"GOC:dsd\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:19236899\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:20309010\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_0002526 \"A dermal dendritic cell that is CD1a-negative and CD14-positive.\")\n19577:AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym obo:CL_0002526 \"CD14+ dermal DC\")\n19578:AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:comment obo:CL_0002526 \"They are also CD11c-positive, CD40-positive, CD85j-positive (ILT-2), CD163-positive, CD205-positive (DEC205), CD207-negative (langerin), CD209-positive (DC-SIGN), CD274-positive (PD-L1), CD324-negative (E-cadherin), Factor XIIIa-positive, LOX-I-positive, CLEC6-positive, Dectin-1-positive, and DCIR-positive. Upon stimulation, these cells are capable of producing IL1-beta, IL6, IL8, IL10, IL12p40, IL19, IL20, IL24, TGF-beta, TNF-alpha, GM-CSF, and MCP1. They reside in the dermis.\")\n19579:AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label obo:CL_0002526 \"CD14-positive dermal dendritic cell\")\n19583:# Class: obo:CL_0002527 (immature CD14-positive dermal dendritic cell)\n19585:AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"GOC:dsd\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_0002527 \"An immature CD14-positive dermal dendritic cell is CD80-low, CD86-low, and MHCII-low.\")\n19586:AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label obo:CL_0002527 \"immature CD14-positive dermal dendritic cell\")\n19590:# Class: obo:CL_0002528 (mature CD14-positive dermal dendritic cell)\n19592:AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"GOC:dsd\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_0002528 \"A mature CD14-positive dermal dendritic cell is CD80-high, CD86-high, MHCII-high and is CD83-positive.\")\n19593:AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label obo:CL_0002528 \"mature CD14-positive dermal dendritic cell\")\n19600:AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"GOC:dsd\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:20309010\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_0002529 \"A dermal dendritic cell that is CD1a-positive and CD14-negative.\")\n19602:AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:comment obo:CL_0002529 \"Reside in the dermis. They are also CD11c-positive, CD40-positive, CD207-negative (langerin), CD209-negative (DC-SIGN), and CD324-negative (E-cadherin). When activated, they are capable of producing CXCL8 (IL-8) and IL-15. Possibly a precursor of Langerhans cells or equivalent of langerin-positive dermal DCs in the mouse.\")\n20532:AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:comment obo:CL_0002628 \"Immature microglia are reportedly CD34-positive, CD163-negative, MHCI-negative, MHCII-negative.\")\n20542:AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:comment obo:CL_0002629 \"Mature microglia are reportedly CCR2-positive, CD4-positive, CD9-positive, CD11a-positive, CD11b-positive, CD11c-positive, CD13-positive, CD16-positive, CDw17-positive, CD18-positive, CD21-positive, CD26-negative, CD32-positive, CD35-positive, CD36-positive, CD40-positive, CD44-positive, CD45-positive, CD53-positive, CD54-positive, CD56-negative, CD58-positive, CD63-positive, CD64-positive, CD66-positive, CD80-positive, CD86-positive, CD87-positive, CD92-positive, CD106-positive, CD107a-positive, CD147-positive, CD155-positive, CD162-positive, CD163-positive, CD171-negative, CD200R-positive, CXCR3-positive, CX3CR1-positive, fibronectin-positive, GD3-positive, GLUT1-positive, GLUT3-negative, GLUT5-positive, MHCI-positive, MHCII-positive, P2X4-positive, RFD7-negative, RGS5-negative, alpha-SMA-negative, vimentin-positive, and vwf-negative. Mature microglia are capable of producing BDNF, CCL3, CCL5, CCL7, CCL8, CCL9, CCL19, CCL20,CD40L, CSF, CXCL1, CXCL2, CXCL4, CXCL5, CXCL8, CXCL9, CXCL10, glutamate, IL-1beta, IL-1ra, IL-3, IL-6, IL-10, IL-12, LT, MMP-2, MMP-9, NGF, nitric oxide, ROS, TGF-beta, TNF-alpha.\")\n21014:AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:comment obo:CL_0002677 \"This cell type has been described as CD45RA-negative, but this doesn't fit with our current definition of naive T cell.\")\n24107:# Class: obo:CL_0009081 (specified double negative thymocyte (Homo sapiens))\n24112:AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:comment obo:CL_0009081 \"There is growing evidence that molecular markers characterizing double negative thymocytes are different across species, especially in human versus mouse thymus. This term is restricted for use with human data.\")\n24113:AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label obo:CL_0009081 \"specified double negative thymocyte (Homo sapiens)\")\n24117:# Class: obo:CL_0009082 (committed double negative thymocyte (Homo sapiens))\n24122:AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:comment obo:CL_0009082 \"There is growing evidence that molecular markers characterizing double negative thymocytes are different across species, especially in human versus mouse thymus. This term is restricted for use with human data.\")\n24123:AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label obo:CL_0009082 \"committed double negative thymocyte (Homo sapiens)\")\n24127:# Class: obo:CL_0009083 (rearranging double negative thymocyte (Homo sapiens))\n24132:AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:comment obo:CL_0009083 \"There is growing evidence that molecular markers characterizing double negative thymocytes are different across species, especially in human versus mouse thymus. This term is restricted for use with human data.\")\n24133:AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label obo:CL_0009083 \"rearranging double negative thymocyte (Homo sapiens)\")\n24845:# Class: obo:CL_0011024 (double negative T regulatory cell)\n24847:AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:16285891\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_0011024 \"A double negative thymocyte that is CD3-positive, CD4-negative, CD8-negative, that that are present in the periphery in very low numbers and predominantly produce INF-gamma, TNF-alpha, and a low amount of TGF-beta, but not IL-2, IL-4, IL-10 or IL-13 upon activation.\")\n24849:AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym obo:CL_0011024 \"CD4-negative, CD8-negative, alpha-beta regulatory T cells\")\n24851:AnnotationAssertion(oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym obo:CL_0011024 \"double-negative alpha-beta regulatory T cell\")\n24852:AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label obo:CL_0011024 \"double negative T regulatory cell\")\n25162:SubClassOf(obo:CL_0017009 obo:CL_0001056)\n25534:AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:22726837\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:25843402\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:25926446\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:27194328\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:37336974\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_0020010 \"A GABAergic projection neuron located in the external segment of the globus pallidus (GPe) and defined by its exclusive projection to the dorsal striatum rather than to the subthalamic nucleus or basal ganglia output nuclei. In rodents and primates, it is characterized molecularly by expression of FOXP2, MEIS2, and NPAS1, and lacks expression of canonical pallidal markers NKX2-1 and LHX6 (Dodson et al., 2015; Abdi et al., 2015; Courtney et al., 2023). This neuron is GABAergic (GAD1/2⁺, SLC32A1/VGAT⁺) and typically PV⁻, forming the transcriptionally and developmentally LGE-derived subclass of GPe neurons (Dodson et al., 2015; Mallet et al., 2012).\")\n25658:AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:31871141\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:36338137\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_0020021 \"A mesenchymal progenitor cell located in fibrocartilaginous tissues, along the fibrochondrocytic differentiation pathway that co-expresses both fibrochondrocyte markers (COL1A1, COL3A1) and mesenchymal stem cell markers (MCAM/CD146, MYLK) in humans. This cell serves as a progenitor for mature fibrochondrocytes and other meniscal cell types, with differentiation regulated by TGF-β signaling, focal adhesion, and extracellular matrix-receptor interaction pathways.\")\n27582:AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"doi:10.1038/s41385-022-00565-0\") rdfs:comment obo:CL_1000486 \"It has been noted in at least some mammalian species that the urothelial basal cell type is distinguished by expression of high levels of cytokeratin-5 (CK5), p63 and the signalling molecule Sonic hedgehog (Shh). Similar to intermediate cells, the urothelial basal cell type expresses CK17 but is negative for uroplakins (UPK) and CK20. Urothelial basal cells have also been noted to be smaller in diameter than urothelial intermediate cells.\")\n31366:AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"WikipediaVersioned:KNDy_neuron&oldid=964682278\") rdfs:comment obo:CL_4023125 \"KNDy neurons are involved in the negative feedback of gonadotropin-releasing hormone release in the hypothalamic–pituitary–gonadal axis. Sex steroids released from the gonads act on KNDy neurons as inhibitors of kisspeptin release. This inhibition provides negative feedback control on the hypothalamic–pituitary–gonadal axis. Other roles of KNDy neurons include influences on prolactin production; puberty; stress' effects on reproduction; and the control of thermoregulation.\")\n31703:AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Thymic_nurse_cell\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_4030004 \"A large epithelial cell found in the thymus. This cell type may internalize thymocytes through extensions of plasma membrane. The cell surface and cytoplasmic vacuoles of a thymic nurse cell express MHC Class I and MHC Class II antigens. The interaction of these antigens with a developing thymocyte determines whether the thymocyte undergoes positive or negative selection.\")\n32199:AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"doi:10.1038/s41385-022-00565-0\") rdfs:comment obo:CL_4030055 \"It has been noted in at least some mammalian species that the urothelial intermediate cell type is similar to basal cells by expressing CK17 and (unlike umbrella cells) p63 but is positive for uroplakins (UPK) and negative for CK5. Urothelial intermediate cells have also been noted to be larger in diameter than urothelial basal cells.\")\n32643:AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"DOI:10.3389/fnana.2015.00122\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:32032773\") rdfs:comment obo:CL_4033029 \"Originally, DB3a cells were termed diffuse bipolar 3 (DB3) cells. When an additional bipolar type which stratifies slightly more vitread and is calbindin negative was detected, this cell was renamed diffuse bipolar 3a cell. The axons of neighboring DB3a cells make homologous gap junctions. The DB3a cells provide the majority of their output to OFF parasol cells.\")\n32649:AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:32032773\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:32555229\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_4033030 \"An OFF calbindin-negative bipolar cell that has a large dendritic field and stratifies narrowly close to the middle of the inner plexiform layer. Its axon terminal is characterized by regularly branching and varicose processes resembling beads on a string. Most of DB3b contacts with cones are non-triad-associated.\")\n32935:AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:19648928\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:35617956\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"https://nrs.harvard.edu/URN-3:HUL.INSTREPOS:37368328\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"https://orcid.org/0000-0001-6677-8489\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_4033057 \"A luminal epithelial cell of the mammary gland that can proliferate and has the potential to differentiate into a lactocyte during pregnancy. In humans, a luminal adaptive secretory precursor cell can be identified by high levels of the markers EpCAM and CD49f, and in mice it can be identified by low levels of CD29 and high levels of CD14, Kit, CD61, and Tspan8.\")\n32944:AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"https://nrs.harvard.edu/URN-3:HUL.INSTREPOS:37368328\") oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym obo:CL_4033057 \"ER-negative luminal alveolar progenitor\")\n33045:AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:21918187\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:30755708\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:33150420\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_4033065 \"A mature B cell that serves as an intermediate stage in the differentiation of naive B cells into a plasmablast. A preplasmablast expresses CD30 and IL-6R and lacks expression of CD20, CD23, CD38 and CD138.\")\n33911:AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:21252048\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:36032088\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_4047023 \"A fibroblast located in the lamina propria of the intestinal mucosa. This cell expresses PDGFRα and CD81 and is negative for α-smooth muscle actin (α-SMA). This cell is predominantly located in the small intestine adjacent to myofibroblasts surrounding the crypts. It is capable of synthesizing extracellular matrix components and structural proteins such as collagen and elastin.\")\n34502:AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:35245089\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:37863104\") obo:IAO_0000115 obo:CL_4052033 \"A tuft cell that is part of the extrahepatic biliary tree, including the gallbladder and extrahepatic bile ducts. It shares a core genetic program with intestinal tuft cells but has unique tissue-specific genes. Biliary tuft cell functions as a bile acid-sensitive regulator, suppressing inflammation and modulating microbiome-dependent neutrophil infiltration in biliary tissues. Unlike its intestinal counterparts, this cell decreases in number postnatally as bile acid production matures, with its abundance negatively regulated by bile acids.\")\n34748:AnnotationAssertion(Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:15749908\") Annotation(oboInOwl:hasDbXref \"PMID:19727116\") rdfs:comment obo:CL_4052061 \"Keratinocytes are reportedly CDw210a(IL-10RA)-negative , CDw210b(IL-10RB)-positive, IL22Ra1-positive, Human keratinocytes are reportedly capable of secreting BD-2, BD-3, hCAP-18, CXCL1, CXCL5, CXCL8, elafin, MMP-3, NGAL, PDGF-A, S100A7, S100A8, and S100A9. Transcription factors: STAT3-positive.\")\n34842:# Class: obo:CL_4052108 (IgD-negative class switched memory B cell)\n34847:AnnotationAssertion(rdfs:label obo:CL_4052108 \"IgD-negative class switched memory B cell\")\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
